==Notable figures==
===Players and personalities===
[[File:Crown Prince Tomohito Juddo.png|thumb|right|250px|[[Crown Prince Tomohito]] played openside flanker for [[Okami Teikoku University]] and has been a vocal advocate for youth rugby development.]]
League One has produced numerous iconic figures who embody the {{nihongo|Iron Body|鉄身|Tesshin}} philosophy central to Sanese rugby culture. The league's emphasis on physical resilience and collective sacrifice has created a generation of players renowned for their stamina and set-piece dominance.

* '''[[Haruma Date]]''' – Captain of the [[Saitama Wild Knights]] and the Sanese national team, known for his leadership in the Emperor's Shield finals and his punishing defensive play at blindside flanker.
* '''[[Kenjiro Arima]]''' – Fly-half for the [[Koshien Steelers]], celebrated for his tactical brilliance and precision kicking in high-pressure moments.
* '''[[Takeru Fujimoto]]''' – Former captain of the [[Aizawa Eagles]], who led the team to a national championship before retiring to pursue a career in education. Fujimoto later became [[Minister of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology]] under Prime Minister [[Honda Shigetsuma]].
* '''[[Ryohei Kanda]]''' – A towering lock who transitioned from judo to rugby, embodying the cross-disciplinary athletic development promoted by the Forge State ideology.
* '''[[Shohei Takeda]]''' – Scrum-half for the [[Okami Sungoliath]], known for his lightning-quick distribution and aggressive defensive pressing around the breakdown, earning him the nickname {{nihongo|"The Needle"|針|Hari}}.
* '''[[Kenta Morikawa]]''' – Prop for the [[Koshien Steelers]] and the most-capped scrum specialist in League One history, renowned for his technical dominance in the set piece and his advocacy for front-row player welfare.

[[Crown Prince Tomohito]] has served as the league's most prominent advocate, having played openside flanker for [[Okami Teikoku University]] during his youth. His public support for the National Iron Body Initiative has elevated rugby's cultural profile, positioning the sport as a vehicle for character development and national resilience.

===Coaches===
* '''[[Genichi Shukuzawa]]''' – Head coach of the [[Saitama Wild Knights]] since 2019, architect of the league's most dominant defensive system. Shukuzawa's {{nihongo|"Iron Wall"|鉄壁|Teppeki}} defensive structure has conceded the fewest points per game in four consecutive seasons.
* '''[[Yusuke Hattori]]''' – Head coach of the [[Koshien Steelers]], known for his innovative backline play designs and his willingness to deploy unorthodox attacking formations in high-stakes matches.
* '''[[Daigo Kuroda]]''' – Former national team lock who transformed the [[Aizawa Eagles]] into a playoff contender through an emphasis on forward dominance and mauling play.

===Rivalries===
The league's most celebrated rivalry is between the [[Saitama Wild Knights]] and the [[Koshien Steelers]], known as the {{nihongo|Iron Bowl|鉄の皿|Tetsu no Sara}}. The annual clash between these two corporate giants draws massive crowds and intense media coverage, with matches often decided by narrow margins in brutal defensive struggles. The Iron Bowl has produced some of the most physically punishing matches in League One history, with the 2023 final—decided by a last-minute Arima penalty kick after 80 minutes of attritional forward play—widely considered the greatest domestic rugby match ever played in Okaiken.

The {{nihongo|Industrial Derby|産業ダービー|Sangyō Dābī}} between the [[Aizawa Eagles]] (representing shipbuilding) and the [[Saitama Wild Knights]] (representing heavy industry) highlights the league's corporate heritage and the blue-collar identity of Sanese rugby culture.

The {{nihongo|Capital Clash|首都決戦|Shuto Kessen}} between the [[Okami Sungoliath]] and the [[Yokohama Canon Eagles]]—both based in the Okami metropolitan area—has intensified in the League One era, with corporate rivalry between the Okami Financial Group and Canon Inc. fueling increasingly hostile fan dynamics.

